rtical line**  \n   \\[\n   L_{2}:\\; x = 1 .\n   \\]\n   This line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore it is **non\u2011sunny**.  \n   It contains the points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((1,3)\\), which are in \\(S\\).\n\n3. **Horizontal line**  \n   \\[\n   L_{3}:\\; y = 1 .\n   \\]\n   This line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence **non\u2011sunny**.  \n   It contains the points \\((2,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\), which are in \\(S\\).\n\nThe three lines are distinct because their equations are different (\\(y=x\\), \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\)).\n\n---\n\n### Verification of the covering condition\n\nEvery point of \\(S\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1,1) &\\in L_{1},\\\\\n(2,2) &\\in L_{1},\\\\\n(1,2) &\\in L_{2},\\\\\n(1,3) &\\in L_{2},\\\\\n(2,1) &\\in L_{3},\\\\\n(3,1) &\\in L_{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus the set \\(\\{L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\}\\) satisfies the required covering property.\n\n---\n\n### Counting sunny lines\n\nAmong the three lines, only \\(L_{1}\\) is sunny; \\(L_{2}\\) and \\(L_{3}\\) are respectively vertical and horizontal, both prohibited directions. Hence the number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k=1\\).\n\n---\n\nSince a configuration with three distinct lines and exactly one sunny line exists, the value \\(k=1\\) is attainable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=1\\text{ is attainable for }n=3}\n\\]"
