-----------------\n### 1.  The non\u2011sunny line must contain three points of \\(S_3\\)\n\nA line of type (2) can intersect the set \\(S_3\\) in at most three points, because the coordinates in \\(S_3\\) satisfy \\(1\\le a,b\\le 3\\).  \nIf the unique non\u2011sunny line among \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) contained only one or two points of \\(S_3\\), then at least five points would remain uncovered.  \n\nA **sunny** line cannot be parallel to any of the three families (2); consequently a sunny line can pass through at most two points of \\(S_3\\) (any three collinear points of \\(S_3\\) lie on a line of type (2)).  \nThus two sunny lines can cover at most \\(2+2=4\\) points, insufficient for the required five.  \n\nHence the non\u2011sunny line must contain exactly three points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The only non\u2011sunny lines with three points of \\(S_3\\)\n\nDirect inspection of (1) shows that the only lines of the forms (2) containing three points of \\(S_3\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x=1},\\qquad\\boxed{y=1},\\qquad\\boxed{x+y=4}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIndeed  \n\n* \\(x=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\);\n* \\(y=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\);\n* \\(x+y=4\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).\n\nAll other lines of type (2) intersect \\(S_3\\) in at most two points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Remaining points cannot be covered by two sunny lines\n\nWe treat the three possibilities (3) separately.\n\n---\n\n#### Case A: non\u2011sunny line \\(x=1\\)\n\nPoints covered: \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \nRemaining points:  \n\n\\[\nR_A=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny line through two points of \\(R_A\\) is\n\n* \\(x=2\\) (vertical) through \\((2,1),(2,2)\\);\n* \\(y=1\\) (horizontal) through \\((2,1),(3,1)\\);\n* \\(x+y=3\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) through \\((2,2),(3,1)\\).\n\nAll three are of the forbidden forms (2); therefore a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(R_A\\).  \nConsequently at least three sunny lines would be needed to cover \\(R_A\\), contradicting the requirement of exactly two sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### Case B: non\u2011sunny line \\(y=1\\)\n\nPoints covered: \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \nRemaining points:  \n\n\\[\nR_B=\\{(1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nPairs of points in \\(R_B\\) give\n\n* \\(x=1\\) (vertical) through \\((1,2),(1,3)\\);\n* \\(y=2\\) (horizontal) through \\((1,2),(2,2)\\);\n* \\(x+y=3\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) through \\((1,3),(2,2)\\).\n\nAgain each is non\u2011sunny, so a sunny line can meet at most one point of \\(R_B\\).  \nThus three sunny lines would be required, impossible.\n\n---\n\n#### Case C: non\u2011sunny line \\(x+y=4\\)\n\nPoints covered: \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \nRemaining points:  \n\n\\[\nR_C=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nPairs of points in \\(R_C\\) give\n\n* \\(x=1\\) (vertical) through \\((1,1),(1,2)\\);\n* \\(y=1\\) (horizontal) through \\((1,1),(2,1)\\);\n* \\(x+y=3\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) through \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).\n\nAll are non\u2011sunny, so again each sunny line can contain at most one of the three points, forcing three sunny lines \u2013 a contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nIn every possible choice for the unique non\u2011sunny line, the three points left uncovered cannot be covered by only two sunny lines. Hence no set of three distinct lines satisfying the conditions with exactly two sunny lines exists for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration with }k=2\\text{ sunny lines is impossible.}}\n\\]"
