"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have proved that for \\(n=3\\) it is impossible to have three distinct lines in the plane with exactly two of them sunny while covering all required points. Hence no construction exists for \\(k=2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. List the six points that must be covered when \\(n=3\\).  \n2. Observe that a non\u2011sunny line must be of the form \\(x=c\\), \\(y=c\\) or \\(x+y=c\\).  \n3. Show that any line of one of these three forms can contain at most three of the six points, and the only lines that actually contain three are  \n   \\[\n   x=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=4 .\n   \\]  \n4. Argue that the unique non\u2011sunny line among the three must contain three points; otherwise the two sunny lines could cover at most four points, leaving at least five uncovered \u2013 impossible.  \n5. For each of the three possible non\u2011sunny lines, examine the three points left uncovered. In every case any line through any two of those points is again of the forbidden forms, so a sunny line can contain at most one of them. Consequently at least three sunny lines would be needed, contradicting the requirement of exactly two sunny lines.  \n6. Conclude that no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for \\(n=3\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\),\n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nWe must find three distinct lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) such that  \n\n* every point of \\(S_3\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly two of the lines are **sunny**, i.e. are not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nA line is **non\u2011sunny** iff it is of one of the three families  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c,\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwith a constant \\(c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The non\u2011sunny line must contain three points of \\(S_3\\)\n\nA line of type (2) can intersect the set \\(S_3\\) in at most three points, because the coordinates in \\(S_3\\) satisfy \\(1\\le a,b\\le 3\\).  \nIf the unique non\u2011sunny line among \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) contained only one or two points of \\(S_3\\), then at least five points would remain uncovered.\n\nA **sunny** line cannot be parallel to any of the three families (2); consequently a sunny line can pass through at most two points of \\(S_3\\).  \nIndeed, any three collinear points of \\(S_3\\) lie on a line of the form (2) (the only triples with this property are listed in \u00a72 below).  \nThus two sunny lines can cover at most \\(2+2=4\\) points, insufficient for the required five.\n\nHence the non\u2011sunny line must contain exactly three points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The only non\u2011sunny lines with three points of \\(S_3\\)\n\nInspecting the six points in (1) shows that the only lines of the forms (2) containing three points are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x=1},\\qquad\\boxed{y=1},\\qquad\\boxed{x+y=4}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIndeed  \n\n* \\(x=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\);\n* \\(y=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\);\n* \\(x+y=4\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).\n\nAll other lines of type (2) intersect \\(S_3\\) in at most two points, and any line not of type (2) can contain at most two points of \\(S_3\\) (otherwise it would be one of the three lines in (3)).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Remaining points cannot be covered by two sunny lines\n\nWe treat the three possibilities (3) separately.\n\n---\n\n#### Case A: non\u2011sunny line \\(x=1\\)\n\nPoints covered: \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\).  \nRemaining points:  \n\n\\[\nR_A=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nAny line through two points of \\(R_A\\) is\n\n* \\(x=2\\) (vertical) through \\((2,1),(2,2)\\);\n* \\(y=1\\) (horizontal) through \\((2,