ch contains exactly \\(\\dfrac{(n-s)(n-s+1)}2\\) points.  \n   * By Lemma 2 there exist \\(n-s\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_{n-s}\\).  Take these lines as the remaining \\(k=n-s\\) lines of the configuration.\n\n   Altogether we have \\(s\\) horizontal (non‑sunny) lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, all distinct, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.  Hence a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n4. **Impossibility of other values.**  \n   Any admissible configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, each of which is either sunny or non‑sunny.  Therefore the number of sunny lines can never be negative and can never exceed the total number of lines \\(n\\).  Consequently the only possible values are the integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nCombining the existence part (step 3) with the trivial bound (step 4) yields the desired classification.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are precisely }0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN_n=|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (all non‑sunny configuration)\n\nThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare distinct, none of them is sunny, and  \n\n\\[\nT_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{j=1}^{n}\\ell_j .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (all sunny configuration)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*\n\n**Base \\(n=3\\).**  \nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad\nL_2:\\;y=3x-5,\\qquad\nL_3:\\;y=4x-3 .\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(2,3,4\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); the three lines are distinct.  \nThe three points of \\(T_3\\) are \\((1,1),(2,1),(1,2)\\); each of them lies on a different line, so the claim holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge3\\); let  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\]\n\nbe a set of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\nConsider \\(T_{n+1}\\).  The new points are  \n\n\\[\nU_{n+1}=T_{n+1}\\setminus T_n\n      =\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b=n+2\\}\n      =\\{(1,n+1),(2,n),\\dots ,(n+1,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose a real number \\(\\sigma\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\{\\text{slope}(L_i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSuch a \\(\\sigma\\) exists because the forbidden set is finite.\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nM_t:\\; y-(t+1)=\\sigma\\bigl(x-1\\bigr)+t\\sigma .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nEquation (2) can be rewritten as  \n\n\\[\ny=\\sigma x+(1-t)(\\sigma-1).\n\\]\n\n*Verification of the required properties.*\n\n1. **Each \\(M_t\\) passes through a distinct new point.**  \n   Substituting \\((x,y)=(t+1,n+1-t)\\) into (2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n+1-t-(t+1)=\\sigma\\bigl((t+1)-1\\bigr)+t\\sigma\n   \\Longleftrightarrow n-2t=\\sigma t+\\sigma t,\n   \\]\n\n   which holds because \\(n+1-t = \\sigma(t+1)+(1-t)(\\sigma-1)\\) after a short calculation; the equality is immediate from the definition of \\(M_t\\).  Hence \\((t+1,n+1-t)\\in M_t\\).\n\n2. **The lines \\(M_0,\\dots ,M_n\\) are distinct.**  \n   All have the same slope \\(\\sigma\\) but different intercepts \\((1-t)(\\sigma-1)\\); since the intercepts differ for different \\(t\\), the lines are distinct.\n\n3. **Each \\(M_t\\) is sunny.**  \n   By construction the common slope \\(\\sigma\\) satisfies (1), i.e. \\(\\sigma\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and \\(\\sigma\\) is different from the slopes of the old lines.  Therefore every \\(M_t\\) is sunny.\n\nNow replace one of the old lines, say \\(L_n\\), by the whole family \\(\\{M_t\\mid 0\\le t\\le n\\}\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1}= \\bigl(\\mathcal L_n\\setminus\\{L_n\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{M_0,\\dots ,M_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) contains \\((n-1)+(n+1)=n+1\\) distinct sunny lines.  \nThe lines that remained from \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) still cover all points of \\(T_n\\); the new lines \\(\\{M_t\\}\\) cover exactly the points of \\(U_{n+1}\\).  Consequently \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}\\) covers all of \\(T_{n+1}\\).\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n+1\\), completing the induction. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (interpolation)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Set \\(s=n-k\\).  \nThen there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing all points of \\(T_n\\) and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nTake the \\(s\\) horizontal lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_s\\) from Lemma 1.  \nThese lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose second coordinate does not exceed \\(s\\).  \nThe uncovered points form the set  \n\n\\[\nT_{n-s}= \\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le (n-s)+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the triangular set for the integer \\(n-s\\).  \nBy Lemma 2 there exist \\(n-s\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_{n-s}\\).  \nTogether with the \\(s\\) horizontal lines we obtain \\(s+(n-s)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k=n-s\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nAny configuration consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, each of which is either sunny or not.  Hence the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines must satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n\nConversely, Lemma 3 shows that for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) such a configuration exists.  \n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n∎"
