k\\) sunny lines exists\u2014remains open.  The difficulty lies in constructing, for an arbitrary \\(k\\), a family of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines that together with \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines cover all points of \\(T_n\\).  The earlier attempt to build such families contained algebraic errors and has not been repaired in the present work.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is called *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n---\n\n### Lemma 1 (Upper bound)\n\nIn any admissible configuration the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  Each line is either sunny or not, so the number of sunny lines cannot be larger than the total number of lines, i.e. \\(k\\le n\\).  Clearly a line cannot be \u201cnegatively\u201d sunny, hence \\(k\\ge 0\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2 (Existence for \\(k=0\\))\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists an admissible configuration with \\(k=0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nThese lines are pairwise distinct and none of them is sunny because all are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis.  \n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  By definition of \\(T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell_b\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nT_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{j=1}^{n}\\ell_j .\n\\]\n\nThus the family \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) satisfies both requirements of the problem and contains no sunny line, i.e. \\(k=0\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n---\n\n### Consequence\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we know that any admissible value of \\(k\\) must lie in the interval \\([0,n]\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 shows that the lower endpoint \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nHence the set of possible values of \\(k\\) is contained in \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and contains the element \\(0\\).  The existence of configurations for the intermediate values \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) remains to be established.  This constitutes the remaining part of the problem. \u220e"

