hosen horizontals because \\(1\\le b\\le n-k\\)).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the selected lines.\n\n5. **Extremal cases.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\):* we keep only the horizontals \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_n\\); none is sunny.  \n   *\\(k=n\\):* we keep only the sunny lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\); no horizontal line is used.  \n   Both are special instances of the general construction.\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\) shows that no larger \\(k\\) is possible, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]"

