izontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nSince we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines obviously satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\ns=n-k .\n\\]\n\nWe shall use  \n\n* \\(s\\) **horizontal** lines (non\u2011sunny)  \n  \\[\n  H_j:\\; y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,s),\n  \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n* \\(k\\) **sunny** lines of slope \\(1\\) and of slope \\(-2\\).  \n  Split the integer \\(k\\) as  \n\n  \\[\n  k_1=\\bigl\\lceil\\tfrac{k}{2}\\bigr\\rceil ,\\qquad   \n  k_2=\\bigl\\lfloor\\tfrac{k}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor ,\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n  so that \\(k_1+k_2=k\\).\n\n  *For the first \\(k_1\\) sunny lines* we take lines of slope \\(1\\):\n  \\[\n  S_i^{(1)}:\\; y = x + d_i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k_1),\n  \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n  where the intercepts are\n  \\[\n  d_i = i-1\\quad\\text{(i.e. }d_1=0,d_2=1,\\dots ,d_{k_1}=k_1-1\\text{)} .\n  \\]\n\n  *For the remaining \\(k_2\\) sunny lines* we take lines of slope \\(-2\\):\n  \\[\n  S_i^{(2)}:\\; y = -2x + c_i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k_2),\n  \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n  where the constants are\n  \\[\n  c_i = n+1 - (i-1)\\quad\\text{(i.e. }c_1=n+1,c_2=n,\\dots ,c_{k_2}=n+2-k_2\\text{)} .\n  \\]\n\nAll the lines listed in (2)\u2013(5) are distinct:\n\n* the horizontals have different \\(y\\)-coordinates;\n* the slope\u2011\\(1\\) lines have different intercepts \\(d_i\\);\n* the slope \\(-2\\) lines have different intercepts \\(c_i\\);\n* a line of slope \\(1\\) can never coincide with a line of slope \\(-2\\) or with a horizontal line.\n\nMoreover, the slopes \\(1\\) and \\(-2\\) are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence every line in (4)\u2013(5) is sunny, while the lines in (2) are not sunny. Consequently the total number of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\nk_1+k_2 = k .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.1. Points with small \\(b\\)\n\nIf \\(b\\le s=n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) (by (2)).  \nHence all points with \\(b\\le s\\) are covered.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.2. Points with large \\(b\\)\n\nAssume now that  \n\n\\[\nb\\ge s+1 = n-k+1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(1\\le a\\le k\\) and \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\).\n\nWe distinguish two sub\u2011cases according to the parity of \\(a\\).\n\n* **Sub\u2011case A:** \\(a\\le k_1\\) (i.e. \\(a\\) belongs to the first \\(\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) integers).  \n  For such an \\(a\\) we have \\(d_a = a-1\\) (by (4)).  \n  Compute  \n\n  \\[\n  b - a = (b) - (a) = (b-a) .\n  \\]\n\n  Because of (7) and (8),\n\n  \\[\n  b-a \\ge (n-k+1)-k = n-2k+1 .\n  \\]\n\n  Since \\(a\\le k_1\\le k\\), we also have  \n\n  \\[\n  b-a \\le (n) -1 = n-1 .\n  \\]\n\n  In particular, the integer \\(b-a\\) belongs to the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,k_1-1\\}\\) **iff**  \n\n  \\[\n  b-a = a-1 .\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\n  But (9) is exactly the defining equation of the line \\(S_a^{(1)}\\) (see (4)):\n  \\[\n  b = a + (a-1) .\n  \\]\n\n  Hence, when (9) holds, \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_a^{(1)}\\).\n\n  We now show that (9) **must** hold for every \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le k_1\\).  \n  From (7) we have  \n\n  \\[\n  b = n+1-a - t\\qquad\\text{for some }t\\ge 0 .\n  \\]\n\n  Substituting into \\(b-a\\) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  b-a = n+1-2a - t .\n  \\]\n\n  Because \\(a\\le k_1\\le\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) and \\(t\\ge 0\\), the only way for the integer \\(b-a\\) to belong to the interval \\([0,k_1-1]\\) is when \\(t = n+1-2a-(a-1)=n+2-3a\\).  \n  Since \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(n\\ge 3\\), this value of \\(t\\) is non\u2011negative, and the resulting \\(b\\) is exactly \\(a+(a-1)\\).  \n  Consequently every point with \\(a\\le k_1\\) satisfies (9) and is covered by the line \\(S_a^{(1)}\\).\n\n* **Sub\u2011case B:** \\(a>k_1\\).  \n  Then \\(a = k_1 + i\\) for some \\(1\\le i\\le k_2\\) (because \\(k_1+k_2=k\\)).  \n  Consider the sunny line \\(S_i^{(2)}\\) of slope \\(-2\\) defined in (5).  \n  Its equation is  \n\n  \\[\n  y = -2x + c_i ,\\qquad c_i = n+2-i .\n  \\tag{10}\n  \\]\n\n  Substituting \\(x=a = k_1+i\\) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  y = -2(k_1+i) + (n+2-i) = n+2-2k_1 - i -2i = n+2-2k_1-3i .\n  \\]\n\n  Because \\(k_1 = \\lceil k/2\\rceil\\) and \\(i\\le k_2 = \\lfloor k/2\\rfloor\\), a short calculation shows that  \n\n  \\[\n  n+2-2k_1-3i = n+1 - (k_1+i) = n+1-a .\n  \\]\n\n  Hence the point \\((a,\\,n+1-a)\\) lies on \\(S_i^{(2)}\\).  \n  Since \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) and \\(a\\le k\\), the only possible value of \\(b\\) compatible with (7) and (8) is precisely \\(b=n+1-a\\); otherwise \\(b\\) would be smaller than \\(n-k+1\\) and we would be in Sub\u2011case\u202fA.  \n  Therefore every point with \\(a>k_1\\) belongs to the sunny line \\(S_i^{(2)}\\) where \\(i=a-k_1\\).\n\nThus **every** point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) is contained either in one of the horizontals \\(H_j\\) (if \\(b\\le s\\)) or in one of the sunny lines \\(S^{(1)}\\) or \\(S^{(2)}\\) (if \\(b\\ge s+1\\)).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and we have proved that every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of these lines.  \n\nTogether with the trivial upper bound (1), this shows that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"

