nes \\(S_i\\) of slope \\(1\\); the horizontals are not sunny. Hence the number of sunny lines is precisely \\(k\\).\n\nSince the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), and the trivial bound (1) shows that no other values are possible, the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nWe must use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, therefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nPut  \n\n\\[\ns=n-k .\n\\]\n\n**(i) Non\u2011sunny lines.**  \nTake the \\(s\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,s).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are distinct and none of them is sunny.\n\n**(ii) Sunny lines.**  \nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y = x + d_i ,\\qquad d_i = s-i+1 = n-k-i+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_i\\) has slope \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence every \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe intercepts \\(d_i\\) are strictly decreasing, so the \\(S_i\\) are distinct.\n\nAltogether we have exactly \\(s+k=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\); thus \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1. Points with small \\(b\\)\n\nIf \\(b\\le s\\) then \\((a,b)\\)