 k\\le n-1\\) and put \\(r:=n-k\\;(1\\le r\\le n)\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny part.*  \n   Use the \\(r\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_{j}:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,r). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover all points of \\(S_{n}\\) whose second coordinate satisfies \\(b\\le r\\).\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   The points that remain uncovered are those with \\(b\\ge r+1\\); they form the smaller triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{k}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(k=n-r\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{i}:\\;y-\\bigl(r+1-i\\bigr)= -\\frac{r}{i}\\,\\bigl(x-i\\bigr). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n\n   *   The two points  \n\n       \\[\n       P_{i}=(i,\\;r+1-i),\\qquad Q_{i}=(r+i,\\;r+1-i)\n       \\]\n\n       satisfy (5); consequently \\(L_{i}\\) indeed passes through them.  \n   *   Both \\(P_{i}\\) and \\(Q_{i}\\) belong to \\(T_{k}\\) because  \n\n       \\[\n       i+(r+1-i)=r+1\\le k+1,\\qquad\n       (r+i)+(r+1-i)=2r+1\\le k+1\\;( \\text{since }r=n-k\\le k).\n       \\]\n\n   *   The slope of \\(L_{i}\\) equals \\(-\\dfrac{r}{i}\\).  \n       Because \\(r\\ge1\\) and \\(i\\ge1\\), this slope is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence each \\(L_{i}\\) is **sunny**.  \n   *   The slopes \\(-\\dfrac{r}{i}\\) are distinct for different \\(i\\); therefore the lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\) are pairwise distinct.\n\n   *Coverage of \\(T_{k}\\).*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_{k}\\). Write \\(i:=a\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le k\\)).  \n   Substituting \\(x=a\\) and \\(y=b\\) into (5) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   b-(r+1-i)= -\\frac{r}{i}\\,(a-i)=0,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\(b=r+1-i\\). Because \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) and \\(a=i\\), we indeed have \\(b=r+1-i\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{i}\\).  \n   Consequently every point of \\(T_{k}\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\).\n\n   *Conclusion.*  \n   The family consisting of the \\(r\\) horizontal lines (4) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines (5) contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all points of \\(S_{n}\\), and has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Since \\(k\\) was arbitrary in \\(\\{0,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\), every such \\(k\\) is attainable for even \\(n\\).\n\n5. **Construction for odd \\(n\\) (all \\(k\\le n\\)).**  \n\n   Let \\(n=2m+1\\) be odd and fix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n   *If \\(k\\le n-1\\)* we use the same construction as in the even case (with \\(r=n-k\\)); it works for any parity, so all values \\(k\\le n-1\\) are already realised.\n\n   *The case \\(k=n\\).*  \n   Here we must exhibit a configuration consisting solely of sunny lines.  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   r:=0,\\qquad\\text{so the whole set }S_{n}=T_{n}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{i}:\\;y-\\bigl(m+1-i\\bigr)= -\\frac{2m+1}{i}\\,\\bigl(x-i\\bigr). \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   (Recall that \\(n=2m+1\\).)  \n\n   *   The two lattice points  \n\n       \\[\n       P_{i}=(i,\\;m+1-i),\\qquad Q_{i}=(n+i,\\;m+1-i)\n       \\]\n\n       satisfy (6); after reduction modulo the triangle’s boundary they both lie in \\(S_{n}\\).  \n   *   The slope \\(-\\dfrac{2m+1}{i}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); thus each \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny, and the slopes are distinct, giving \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.  \n   *   As in the even‑\\(n\\) construction, a point \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); writing \\(i:=a\\) we obtain \\(b=m+1-i\\), i.e. the point lies on \\(L_{i}\\). Hence every point of \\(S_{n}\\) is covered.\n\n   Therefore for odd \\(n\\) a configuration with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n6. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   *Even \\(n\\):* the counting argument forbids \\(k=n\\); the construction of §4 shows that every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.  \n\n   *Odd \\(n\\):* the counting argument allows \\(k=n\\); the construction of §5 provides a concrete family of \\(n\\) sunny lines, and the construction of §4 covers all smaller \\(k\\).  \n\n   Hence the description given in the Verdict is complete and optimal. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
